WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:03.336
So what is cryptography?

00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:07.799
Cryptography is a field
that deals with securing

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:10.135
communication and computation.

00:00:10.135 --> 00:00:13.138
We want to ensure both confidentiality
and integrity.

00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:16.141
Today cryptography is 
used all over the place.

00:00:16.141 --> 00:00:19.144
The users often don't even know
they're using cryptography,

00:00:19.144 --> 00:00:21.491
your cellphone uses it all the time.

00:00:21.491 --> 00:00:25.150
Anything you do online has cryptography in it.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.070
In cryptography, what motivates us
is the real world problems.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.613
So what do we study?

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:31.364
Whatever the world

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:34.367
kind of throws at us.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:37.287
My name is Yael Tauman Kalai

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:39.601
I'm a researcher at Microsoft

00:00:39.601 --> 00:00:44.085
and an adjunct professor at M.I.T.

00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:47.756
My research focuses
on theoretical cryptography in particular.

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:49.257
But more largely

00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:52.260
I'm interested
in theoretical computer science.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.513
So people use cryptography
for thousands of years

00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:58.475
and they often just came up with schemes
and hope

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:01.478
they were secure
and everything was secret about them.

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:03.229
One key takeaway

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.690
about modern cryptography,
as opposed to like

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.693
the classical cryptography
that was used thousands of years ago,

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:12.363
Is that today, actually,
all our algorithms are public.

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:15.784
The way I think of it,
whereas cryptography used to be an art,

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:17.452
now it became a science.

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:21.164
When modern cryptography started,

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:24.099
the emphasis was on 
securing communication.

00:01:26.076 --> 00:01:28.932
You know, a message was sent. 
you want to make sure it's secret.

00:01:28.932 --> 00:01:32.467
You want to make sure it's not tampered with.
You want to make sure it's authentic.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:37.044
So our goal is to kind of encrypt
this message.

00:01:37.056 --> 00:01:39.631
So we nailed that part
that's, that's working well.

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:43.895
Now we're dealing a lot with securing computation.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:47.565
There was time
where smartphones started to come out.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:49.692
And later smartwatches came out.

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:52.695
All of a sudden
we had a lot of little devices

00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:55.740
that interact with the real world
and need to do computations.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:57.784
But they're very weak devices.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:59.631
So a natural way to think about is,

00:01:59.631 --> 00:02:01.765
okay, they'll ask some, the desktop

00:02:01.765 --> 00:02:03.736
or some cloud computing or whatever,

00:02:03.736 --> 00:02:06.837
a stronger computation device to do the work for it.

00:02:06.837 --> 00:02:09.788
So now with abundance of weak devices

00:02:09.788 --> 00:02:13.093
delegating computations off to a cloud computer,

00:02:13.093 --> 00:02:15.986
there's two main challenges that come about.

00:02:15.986 --> 00:02:17.607
First, maybe I want privacy.

00:02:17.607 --> 00:02:21.328
I don't want to let the server know
what I'm doing,

00:02:21.328 --> 00:02:24.688
Another question, which is kind of very, very dear
to my heart,

00:02:24.688 --> 00:02:26.693
and I spent a lot of time working on it,

00:02:26.693 --> 00:02:29.524
is exactly ensuring the integrity of the result.

00:02:29.607 --> 00:02:32.068
How do we ensure that the result
we get is actually correct?

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:36.489
How can we do that in a verifiable way?

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.952
So the computational heavy device
that generates this computation,

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:45.123
we want that device
not only to give the output, but also

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:49.294
to give kind of a succinct certificate
that certifies the correctness

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:52.630
of this output.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.426
In cryptography,
or in computer science in general,

00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:59.262
the way we think about proofs
really evolved.

00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:04.058
There is work from the 80s
where the goal was to come up with proofs

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:07.645
that reveal no information
beyond the validity of the statement.

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:10.815
And the first step to
that is the definition of what's

00:03:10.815 --> 00:03:13.776
called 'interactive proofs'.

00:03:13.776 --> 00:03:14.903
We have a user.

00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:20.116
He has a, like, a little smartphone.
And then we have, like, a powerful server,

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:21.784
think of it as the cloud.

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:24.370
This is your prover
and this is your verifier.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.580
He's weak.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.581
He's strong.

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:29.250
You want the strong cloud
to do a computation for you.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.589
So what interactive proofs gives us is two things.

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:39.385
First, It allows us to prove things
without giving out information

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:41.221
beyond the validity of the statement.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:44.000
How to do this with interaction?

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.351
Here's like message one,
he asks question and so on, so forth.

00:03:46.684 --> 00:03:49.479
So the power of interactive proofs
comes from the fact that

00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:52.440
these questions have..are randomized.

00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:55.266
But the other thing interactive proofs
give us

00:03:55.266 --> 00:03:57.799
is it gives us a way to prove a statement,

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:00.657
in a very succinct way,

00:04:00.657 --> 00:04:05.411
using much less communication, or much less bits.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:09.415
So while interactive proofs
are very advantageous

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:12.585
in that they allow you to hide information
and they allow

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:14.379
for much more succinct proofs,

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:16.339
there are inherent disadvantages.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.800
The main disadvantage
is that an interactive proof is

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.635
not transferable.

00:04:20.635 --> 00:04:23.680
Once you eliminate interaction,
there is no real proof

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.975
that you still have the security guarantee
that the interactive protocol had.

00:04:27.183 --> 00:04:29.519
Let's say
we only care about the succinctness,

00:04:29.519 --> 00:04:32.438
the fact that the proof is short
and can be efficiently verified.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:35.817
You really want to have a certificate
saying that this,

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:38.392
that, you know, indeed this is a valid transaction,

00:04:38.392 --> 00:04:40.622
assuming you can't check it on your own.

00:04:40.622 --> 00:04:43.950
So how do we go from here
to a actual certificate?

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:45.243
What do we do?

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:48.385
We apply what's called Fiat-Shamir paradigm

00:04:48.385 --> 00:04:50.665
or also known as Fiat-Shamir heuristic.

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:54.794
You can take any interactive proof

00:04:55.086 --> 00:04:59.882
in which the verifier's messages
are just random

00:04:59.882 --> 00:05:02.119
and convert them into not interactive ones.

00:05:02.119 --> 00:05:06.347
Ao they can essentially eliminate interactions using cryptography.

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:08.516
What we get by applying Fiat-Shamir

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:11.066
is what's called a succinct, because it's short...

00:05:11.066 --> 00:05:13.568
non-interactive...
because we eliminate interaction...

00:05:13.568 --> 00:05:14.319
argument.

00:05:14.355 --> 00:05:18.901
And the the acronym for this is SNARG,
succint non-interactive argument.

00:05:19.193 --> 00:05:20.987
One main place where SNARGs,

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:22.530
or in other words, a certificate that

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.907
certifies
the correctness of the computation,

00:05:24.907 --> 00:05:27.073
are used a lot is in the blockchain.

00:05:27.073 --> 00:05:30.747
Today, Etherium are using SNARGS

00:05:30.747 --> 00:05:31.956
as a way

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:34.751
to certify the validity

00:05:34.751 --> 00:05:36.461
of the transactions

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:39.005
We're trying to simulate
this arbitrarily complicated function.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.799
G and fool distinguishers in this class F.

00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:44.802
Therefore you need a SNARG

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:45.892
so that, exactly, exactly.

00:05:45.892 --> 00:05:48.390
So, what are the biggest problems
in cryptography today?

00:05:50.224 --> 00:05:52.810
If I had to think of the new biggest problem,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:56.731
one of them has to do with quantum computers.

00:05:56.814 --> 00:05:59.025
So cryptography is based

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:00.735
on computational assumptions.

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:02.987
The security of our schemes are based

00:06:02.987 --> 00:06:04.530
on some hardness assumptions.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:08.659
We believe that it's hard, for example,
to factor very, very large numbers.

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:11.621
These assumptions often no longer hold

00:06:11.621 --> 00:06:14.082
once we have large scale
quantum computers,

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:15.461
they could break our assumptions

00:06:15.461 --> 00:06:18.461
And hence render our schemes completely insecure.

00:06:18.461 --> 00:06:21.214
However, contrary to the narrative

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:24.482
about doom, I actually feel
a narrative of excitement!

00:06:24.482 --> 00:06:28.638
Of, oh, let's see what a quantum computer brings,
like what capability.

00:06:29.806 --> 00:06:33.267
We can upgrade most of our schemes to be

00:06:33.351 --> 00:06:34.852
post-quantum secure.

00:06:34.852 --> 00:06:35.728
We're still working on it

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:37.730
but you know, we've made a very large

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.014
advancement on this front.

00:06:39.014 --> 00:06:40.191
And that's pretty exciting.

